%% The Topic 6 revision sheet — included by KB Part Five and by the RN volume. Must make sense on its own.

## 1. Governance and why gender matters

- **Governance** — the manner in which power is used to manage a country's resources (**World Bank 1992**); wider than government: state, markets, parties, civil society, *jirgas*. Popularised after the World Bank's **1989** "crisis of governance".
- **Good governance** (UNESCAP, eight features) — **participation, rule of law, transparency, responsiveness, consensus orientation, equity and inclusiveness, effectiveness and efficiency, accountability**. Participation of **women and men** is part of the definition.
- **Gender and governance** — two questions: are women **present** where decisions are made, and do **outcomes** serve women? Parts: participation; institutions (NCSW **2000**, caucuses); gender-responsive budgeting; accountability (sex-disaggregated data).
- **Four arguments** — **justice** (half the citizens), **interests** (distinct needs), **resource** (talent), **symbolic** (role models).
- **Rights basis** — UDHR **Art. 21** (1948); ICCPR **Art. 25** (1966); CEDAW **Art. 7** (political life) and **Art. 4(1)** (temporary special measures); Constitution Arts **17, 25, 32, 34, 51, 59, 106**; SDG **5.5**.


- **Government vs governance** — government = institutions holding state power (parliament, cabinet, courts, civil service); governance = the whole **process** of managing public affairs, incl. **markets, parties, civil society, media, *jirgas***. UNDP **1997**: authority exercised so citizens **articulate interests, exercise rights, meet obligations, mediate differences**.
- **Village pump example** — an all-male council sites the pump near the mosque or market; women who fetch water want it near homes: **who sits at the table changes what is decided**.
- **Pakistan's gender machinery** — Women's Division **1979** → Ministry of Women Development → devolved to provinces after the **18th Amendment (2010)** (Women Development Departments); federal **Ministry of Human Rights (2015)**; **NCSW 2000** (Act **2012**); provincial commissions (e.g. Punjab **2014**); **National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women (2002)**; **Gender Reform Action Plan (GRAP)** (early 2000s — women's share of public jobs; gender-sensitive departments); Finance Ministry **Gender Responsive Budgeting Initiative**; harassment ombudspersons (**2010**) and property ombudsperson (**2020**). Verdict: the **machinery** exists; **resources and political will** are weak.
- **Governance spaces beyond parliament** — executive (zero women federal ministers, **2025**); bureaucracy (**10%** quota; few in top grades); judiciary (**Ayesha Malik**, first woman SC judge, **2022**); local government (**33%**, 2000–01); party executives (decide tickets and lists); informal *jirgas* and *panchayats* (all-male; *vani*, *swara*, honour punishments).
- **Jinnah (Aligarh, 10 March 1944)** — "No nation can rise to the height of glory unless your women are side by side with you" — opener for any participation answer.

## 2. Representation theory

- **Pitkin 1967** — **formal** (rules), **descriptive** (resemble), **substantive** (act for), **symbolic** (stand for).
- **Phillips 1995** — "**politics of presence**": who represents matters, not only ideas.
- **Kanter 1977** — **tokens** under ~15%: visible, isolated, pressured.
- **Dahlerup 1988** — **critical mass** (~30%); also **critical acts**; "**fast track**" (quotas) vs "**incremental track**" (Nordic).
- **Childs and Krook 2009** — **critical actors** matter more than numbers.
- Targets: ECOSOC **30%** (1990); Beijing Critical Area **G** (1995).
- Use: "Quotas delivered **descriptive** representation; the question is **substantive** representation."


- **Pitkin applied to Pakistan** — **formal**: reserved seats from party lists, general seats by FPTP; **descriptive**: about one-fifth women vs half the population; **substantive**: laws of 2010–2016; **symbolic**: Benazir Bhutto (1988), Maryam Nawaz (2024).
- **Critical mass — balanced view** — *for*: more women reduce tokenism, permit alliances, normalise women's issues (Nordic parliaments, Rwanda); *against*: women divided by party, class, ideology; a few determined women can achieve much; party discipline limits all members. **Line**: numbers create the **opportunity**; **critical actors**, cross-party caucuses and party support turn it into change (Pakistan's **Women's Parliamentary Caucus, 2008**).
- **Mansbridge (1999)** — descriptive representation matters most where groups have been distrusted or where interests are "uncrystallised" (not yet on the agenda) — e.g. domestic violence before women entered parliament.
- **Using theory in answers** — open with a definition of governance or representation, then frame the judgement: "Quotas have delivered **descriptive** representation; the question is **substantive** representation."

## 3. The suffrage movement (2019 note)

- **Suffrage** = right to vote. **Suffragists** (peaceful; NUWSS **1897**, **Millicent Fawcett**) vs **suffragettes** (militant; WSPU **1903**, **Emmeline Pankhurst**, "Deeds, not words"; word coined by the *Daily Mail* **1906**).
- **Against women's votes** — separate spheres, coverture, "nature", social order. **For** — natural rights, democracy (no taxation without representation), social improvement.
- **USA** — London 1840 (Stanton and Mott excluded) → **Seneca Falls 1848** (Declaration of Sentiments; Douglass) → 15th Amendment 1870 split → **NWSA** (Stanton, Anthony) and **AWSA** (Stone) **1869** → Wyoming **1869** → **NAWSA 1890** → Alice Paul, pickets 1917 → **19th Amendment 1920** → Voting Rights Act 1965 for Black women in the South.
- **Britain** — Wollstonecraft 1792 → **Mill's petition 1866**, *Subjection* 1869 → NUWSS 1897 → WSPU 1903 → hunger strikes, **Cat and Mouse Act 1913**, **Emily Davison 1913** → war work → **1918** (over 30, property) → **1928** (21, equal).
- **World** — **New Zealand 1893** (Kate Sheppard; MCQ 2024); Australia 1902; **Finland 1906** (first to stand); Norway 1913; Russia 1917; Germany 1918; USA 1920; Ceylon **1931**; France 1944; India and Pakistan 1947; Switzerland 1971; Saudi Arabia 2015 (municipal).
- **South Asia** — 1917 Women's Indian Association deputation to Montagu (Sarojini Naidu); **Madras 1921**; **1935 Act** (6 million women; reserved seats); Muslim League women's sub-committee 1938; Pakistan **1947** adult franchise; first CA **two** women.
- **Assessment** — necessary not sufficient; class and race limits; led to the second wave; Pakistan still has a 9.94m registration gap.


- **Suffrage in the Muslim world** — **Azerbaijan 1918** (often cited first); **Turkey** local **1930**, national **1934** (18 women MPs in 1935); **Indonesia 1945**; **Pakistan 1947**; **Bangladesh 1971**; **Iran 1963**; **Kuwait 2005**; **Saudi Arabia 2015** (municipal). Many Muslim-majority states enfranchised women **before Switzerland (1971)**; Pakistan, Bangladesh, Turkey and Indonesia have had women heads of government.
- **Short-note frame (2019)** — meaning (suffragist vs suffragette) → origins (Wollstonecraft, Seneca Falls) → USA (NWSA/AWSA 1869, NAWSA 1890, 19th Amendment 1920) → Britain (Mill 1866, NUWSS 1897, WSPU 1903, 1918, 1928) → world (NZ 1893, Finland 1906) → South Asia (1917, Madras 1921, 1935 Act, Pakistan 1947) → assessment (necessary, not sufficient).
- **Why the vote mattered** — once women voted, legislators in Britain and the US began passing laws on married women's property, custody, maternity and working conditions.
- **Limits** — white, middle-class leadership; racism (Black women in the US South until **1965**); class (Britain's 1918 property rule); formal rights without social equality → second wave.

## 4. Women as voters

- **Law** — vote at **18** (from 21 in **2002**; MCQ 2016); CNIC (NADRA) needed; ECP prepares the roll.
- **2024 data** — 128.58m voters; **69.26m men, 59.32m women**; gap **9.94m (7.7%)**, down from **12.8% (2013)**. Turnout **43% women vs 52% men** (gap 9 points, 10 in 2018); women cast **24.4m** of 58.9m votes (+2.7m); widest gap **KP** (15.7), Sindh 7.4.
- **Registration barriers** — no CNIC, documents, distance, cost, family control, awareness.
- **Turnout barriers** — family decides; **local agreements** barring women; distance and security; few female polling stations and staff; care work; information.
- **Remedies** — **Elections Act 2017 s. 9**: poll void if women's turnout **< 10%**; special measures where the registration gap > 10%; Lower Dir by-election annulled **2015**; NADRA mobile vans, women's counters; Aurat Foundation, FAFEN.
- **Autonomy** — family and *biradari* block voting; three steps: on the list, at the booth, own choice. BISP (2008) as courting women voters.


- **Why the CNIC matters beyond voting** — no CNIC = no vote, bank account, **BISP** payment, SIM card, inheritance claim or passport; BISP (**2008**, paid only to women with CNICs) was a major driver of women's registration — social policy and participation reinforce each other.
- **Violence and intimidation** — threats and rumours keep women home; women's polling stations may be targeted; militants have warned women not to vote; women polling staff are harassed. Remedies: safe nearby women's stations, women police, security.
- **Women voting differently?** — in the West a "gender gap" in party preference emerged from the 1980s (US women more Democratic; young women to the left of young men); in Pakistan family voting blurs it, but parties increasingly court women (BISP, provincial schemes).
- **Balance** — progress: gap 12.8% (2013) → 7.7% (2024); +2.7m women's votes; s. 9 deters open bans. Problems: 9.94m unregistered; 43% vs 52% turnout; family voting. **Line**: right secure, access improving, **autonomy** the deepest challenge.

## 5. Women as candidates (2024)

- **Pipeline** — eligible → aspirants → party tickets → winners.
- **Data** — **306** women contested NA general seats in 2024; **11** won (5 independents, 4 PML-N, 2 PPP); **8** in 2018; **5** (10 with by-elections) in 2013. 11 of 266 ≈ **4%**.
- **Law** — age **25**; BA condition 2002–08; **Elections Act s. 206**: **5%** of general-seat tickets to women (no rule on winnable seats).
- **Barriers** — **party** gatekeeping and "electables"; **money** (costly campaigns, little female property); **social** norms (purdah, honour, consent, care); **violence and harassment** (online abuse; Benazir assassinated **27 Dec 2007**); **FPTP** system and *biradari*; **dynasty** as the main route (Benazir, Maryam Nawaz).
- **Fewer aspire** — socialisation ("politics is dirty"), less encouragement.
- **Reforms** — raise and enforce the ticket quota in winnable seats; sanctions; campaign finance; women in party bodies; safety; local government as a pathway.


- **Local government as pipeline** — LGO **2001**: **33%** of union, tehsil and district seats; ~**36,000** women elected (**2000–01**); some seats unfilled in conservative districts; proxies; but confidence, experience and respect gained; some moved to provincial seats; provincial LG laws of the 2010s kept women's seats at lower shares.
- **Online harassment** — trolling, doctored images, sexual slurs and threats against women candidates; **PECA 2016**; **Digital Rights Foundation** helpline (**2016**); weak enforcement; abuse drives some women out of politics.
- **Fewer aspire** — women less often encouraged to run, more likely to doubt qualifications, more worried about family effects; "politics is dirty"; mentoring and encouragement raise ambition.
- **Cases** — Benazir Bhutto (PPP chair; PM **1988–90**, **1993–96**; assassinated **27 Dec 2007**, Rawalpindi) — possibility and danger; 11 women on general seats in **2024** = about **4%** of 266.
- **Dynasty balance** — critics: proxies; defenders: many became leaders in their own right; male dynasties common too. **Line**: a **route, not a disqualification** — the problem is that it is almost the **only** route.

## 6. Women as representatives (2022)

- **Seats** — NA **336** (266 general, **60** women, 10 non-Muslim); Senate **96** (**17** women; 18 in 2024); Punjab **66**, Sindh **29**, KP **26**, Balochistan **11**. Women ≈ **one-fifth** of the NA.
- **How reserved seats are filled** — PR from party priority lists, in proportion to general seats won; independents who join a party count. 2024–25 Supreme Court dispute over PTI-backed independents' share.
- **Two classes** — general-seat women (constituency, funds) vs reserved-seat women (no constituency, party-dependent, "second-class").
- **Issues** — legitimacy ("nominated"); no constituency; party control and relatives on lists; unequal development funds and offices; soft portfolios, few committee chairs; **zero women ministers (2025)**; sexist remarks and online abuse; family duties and late sessions; capacity; few move to general seats.
- **Response** — **Women's Parliamentary Caucus 2008** (Fahmida Mirza) → laws of 2010–2016; FAFEN/PILDAT find women among the most active members.
- **Firsts** — 1947 Shahnawaz and Ikramullah; 1965 Fatima Jinnah; 1973 Ra'ana Liaquat (governor); 1988 Benazir (PM); 2008 Fahmida Mirza (Speaker); 2011 Hina Rabbani Khar (FM); 2017 Tehmina Janjua (foreign secretary); 2022 Ayesha Malik (SC); 2024 Maryam Nawaz (CM).
- **Reforms** — constituencies or direct election; transparent lists; equal resources; key committees and ministries; codes of conduct; day-care; training.


- **Senate and provinces** — Senate's **17** women's seats filled by assemblies by **single transferable vote** (party strength again); Senator **Sughra Imam** moved the anti-honour-killing bill (**2014**); provincial caucuses pushed DV laws — Sindh **2013**, Balochistan **2014**, Punjab **2016**, KP **2021**; after the 18th Amendment provincial women legislators matter more for social policy.
- **Everyday dependence** — reserved-seat members often must approach a male general-seat colleague to get a tube well, school repair or road for the women who contact them.
- **Effective or not?** — yes: caucus laws, active in debates and questions, violence and harassment became parliamentary business; limited: dependent on leaders, few in real power, few move to general seats, economic policy still male. **Line**: real difference on women's issues despite structural constraints.
- **Interim reform** — assign reserved-seat members districts with equal development funds until direct election is introduced.

## 7. Quotas — types, pros and cons (2016, 2020, 2021, 2024)

- **Definition** — minimum number or share of women; **temporary special measures** (CEDAW 4(1)); Constitution **Art. 25(3)**.
- **Types** — **reserved seats** (result: Pakistan 60, Bangladesh 50, Rwanda); **legislated candidate quotas** (opportunity: Pakistan 5%, Nepal, France parity 2000; **zipper** lists); **voluntary party quotas** (Sweden, Norway). Also local (India 1992 one-third; Pakistan 33% 2001), boards (Norway **40%** 2003), jobs (Pakistan 10%).
- **World** — 130+ countries; women ≈ **27%** of parliaments; Rwanda **60%+**.
- **Pros** — fast track (Dahlerup); compensates barriers, not merit; justice and descriptive representation; better policy (Chattopadhyay and Duflo); role models; critical mass; training ground.
- **Cons** — merit; tokens and proxies (*biwi-beti-behen*); stigma; ceiling not floor; elite capture; weak voter link; essentialism; symptoms not causes.
- **Answering merit** — merit already distorted by money and *biradari*; many quota women highly educated; diversity improves decisions; Sweden's quotas pushed out "**mediocre men**" (Besley et al. 2017).
- **Design** — legal; placement in winnable seats; sanctions; transparent selection; direct election; review.
- **Verdict** — necessary but not sufficient; design decides.


- **"Quotas are a Western idea" — answered** — reserved seats date from the **1935 Act** and Pakistan's own constitutions (1956, 1962, 1973); Muslim-majority users: **Bangladesh** (50 seats), **Indonesia** (30% candidate quota), **Tunisia** (parity lists, **2011**), **Afghanistan** (about a quarter of lower-house seats under the **2004** constitution, lost after **2021**); the **UK** and **US** have **no** national legislated quotas. The debate is about **design**, not culture.
- **Simple words** — a quota does not lower the bar; it removes a hidden bar that was already there.
- **"Pull each other up" link (2020)** — mentoring, sponsoring and networking within fair procedures = removing barriers men's networks never faced, not preferential treatment.
- **Answering 2016/2021** — define + types → pros → cons → Pakistan's experience (history; 60; 5%; caucus laws; but 11 general-seat women) → reforms → balanced conclusion; cite Dahlerup (fast track) and Pitkin.

## 8. History of Pakistan's quota (2025)

- **1935** Act — reserved seats; Jahanara Shahnawaz (Round Table Conferences; Punjab Assembly 1937).
- **1947** — first CA **2 of 69** women.
- **1956** — **10** seats (5 East, 5 West). **1962** — **6** seats (3 + 3), indirect.
- **1973** — **10** NA seats for 10 years or three elections, indirect.
- **1985** — raised to **20** (Zia; alongside Hudood 1979, Evidence 1984).
- **1988–2002** — **lapse**: 1990, 1993, 1997 with no reserved seats; 2–6 women; over 97% male — the strongest case for quotas.
- **2000–01** — Devolution Plan / LGO 2001: **33%** local (MCQ 2016); ~**36,000** women councillors.
- **2002** — **LFO**: NA enlarged to **342**, **60** women's seats (≈ **17%**; 2021 question; MCQ 2016), Senate **17**, provinces **128**; validated by **17th Amendment 2003**; NCSW 2000; National Policy 2002; women's share from <3% to >20%.
- **2008** — Women's Parliamentary Caucus. **2010** — 18th Amendment keeps seats. **2013+** — provincial LG laws reduce local share. **2017** — Elections Act: **5%** tickets (s. 206), **10%** turnout (s. 9). **2018** — 25th Amendment: NA **336**, 60 kept; KP provincial seats rise.
- **Assessment** — symbolic (1956–88) → substantial (2002) → not yet empowering; next step direct election and stronger candidate quotas.


- **Allocation worked example** — NA women's seats by province after the 2023 delimitation: **Punjab 32, Sindh 14, KP 10, Balochistan 4**; within a province, seats in proportion to general seats won (**Art. 51(6)(d)**); independents joining a party within three days count. A party winning half of Punjab's general seats gets ≈16 of its 32 women's seats; a party with no general seats gets none.
- **Consequences** — reserved-seat women loyal to leaders who set list order; seats become a prize in membership disputes (**2024–25**).
- **The 1990s lesson** — with **2–6** women in a house of 200+, the NA was over **97%** male; the lapse is the strongest historical case for quotas.
- **Assessment frame** — 1956–88 **symbolic** (few, indirect seats); 2002 onwards **substantial** (60, 17, 132; 5% tickets); not yet **empowering** (indirect, party-controlled).
- **Timeline to draw** — 1935 → 1947 → 1956 → 1962 → 1973 → 1985 → lapse 1988–2002 → 2000–01 → 2002 → 2008 → 2010 → 2017 → 2018.

## 9. Impact of women representatives (2017, 2018, 2021)

- **Four impacts** — policy, process, symbolic, democratic.
- **World evidence** — **Chattopadhyay and Duflo 2004** (India; women heads invested in **drinking water**, roads in West Bengal); **Beaman et al. 2009/2012** (less voter bias; higher girls' aspirations).
- **Pakistan evidence** — laws: **2006** Women's Protection Act; **2010** harassment and s. 509; **2011** acid and anti-women practices (private members' bills); **2012** NCSW Act; **2016** honour and rape; provincial DV laws 2013–2021; women among the most active members; agenda change; BISP 2008; 2001 councillors mixed.
- **Democracy and development (2017)** — legitimacy, consensus (cross-party caucus), peace (**UNSCR 1325**, 2000); spending on health, education, water; women's economic rights (property 2020, HBW Sindh 2018); LFPR 24.4%. **Realities** limiting it: poverty, literacy, patriarchy, feudal and tribal power, dynasties, military interventions, party dependence.
- **Equal numbers (2018)** — "**women hold up half the sky**" (Mao); for: justice, interests, France parity 2000, Rwanda; against: merit, women not one group, proxies; middle: parity of opportunity, quotas as a means; SDG 5.5.
- **Limits** — party discipline, dependence, elite composition, marginal posts, implementation gap, backlash (CII vs Punjab 2016 law).


- **Honour-killing law as a case of critical actors** — Sughra Imam's bill (**2014**) passed the Senate (**2015**), stalled in the NA; after Qandeel Baloch's murder (**July 2016**) women legislators, the caucus, civil society and media pressed again; a **joint sitting** passed the Act in **October 2016** (life sentence even if the family forgives).
- **Local councillors (2001–05)** — mixed: many proxies and excluded from funds; others raised water, girls' schools and health, gained respect, moved up.
- **UNSCR 1325 (2000)** — women's participation in peace and security; agreements more durable when women take part.
- **Parity examples** — France's parity law (**2000**); Rwanda's majority-women parliament; Nepal **35%** (2026).
- **Answering 2017** — use the cycle (legitimate democracy → laws and budgets for health, education, water → women's work and property → growth → educated next generation → more demand for women's voice), then the **socio-economic realities** that limit it.

## 10. Women in leadership (2020)

- **Pakistan** — Benazir (1988–90, 1993–96); Maryam Nawaz (2024); Ayesha Malik (2022); Fahmida Mirza (2008); Hina Rabbani Khar (2011); Tehmina Janjua (2017); Lt Gen **Nigar Johar** (2020); **zero women ministers (2025)**; 10% job quota; women's police stations (1994); SECP: one woman director.
- **Metaphors** — **glass ceiling** (1980s); **sticky floor**; **leaky pipeline**; **labyrinth** (Eagly and Carli 2007); **glass cliff** (Ryan and Haslam 2005; MCQ 2026); **queen bee** (Staines, Tavris, Jayaratne 1974).
- **Barriers** — stereotypes and the **double bind**; old boys' networks; care; subjective promotion; harassment; few role models.
- **"Pull each other up" — useful** — role models, mentoring, **sponsorship**, networks, family-friendly policies; mirrors men's networks; ends tokenism; India's role-model evidence; the caucus.
- **Against** — merit, resentment and stigma, extra burden on women, assumes solidarity, queen bee reality.
- **Line** — corrective, not preferential, when done within fair, transparent procedures and backed by institutional reform.


- **Double bind in Pakistan** — tough women called arrogant, gentle women weak; dress, family life and marital status scrutinised; Benazir faced claims that a woman could not head a Muslim state; Maryam Nawaz similar comment; women in bureaucracy and judiciary must work harder to be taken seriously.
- **Benazir's openings** — women's police stations (**1994**), First Women Bank (**1989**), women judges in the high courts — doors opened, standards not lowered.
- **Over-mentored, under-sponsored** — women receive advice but rarely the backing of a senior person who puts their name forward; sponsorship drives promotion.
- **Simple words** — men have ropes handed down from the top; a woman who reaches the top and lowers a rope gives others what men already had.
- **Answering 2020** — barriers → case for (role models, mentoring, sponsorship, networks, tokenism, India's evidence, the caucus) → case against (merit, stigma, burden, solidarity assumed, queen bee) → Pakistan's leaders → line: corrective with institutional reform.

## 11. South Asian case studies

- **Paradox** — many dynastic women leaders (Bandaranaike **1960**, Indira Gandhi **1966**, Benazir **1988**, Khaleda Zia **1991**, Kumaratunga **1994**, Hasina **1996**) but low ordinary participation.
- **India** — vote from 1950; **73rd/74th Amendments 1992**: one-third local (many states 50%); Lok Sabha ~**14%** (74 women, 2024); **106th Amendment 2023** — one-third after census and delimitation (not before **2029**); *sarpanch-pati* proxies.
- **Bangladesh** — Khaleda and Hasina 1991–2024; Hasina ousted **Aug 2024**; reserved seats 15 (1972), 30 (1979), lapse, 45 (2004), **50 (2011)**; **2026**: 85 women contested, **7** won directly, **57 of 350** total; Union Parishad women's seats directly elected since **1997**.
- **Sri Lanka** — universal franchise **1931**; Bandaranaike 1960; Kumaratunga president 1994–2005; **Harini Amarasuriya** PM **2024**; ~5% women for decades; record **22 of 225** (2024); **25%** local quota (2018).
- **Nepal** — **2015** constitution: one-third federal; president and vice-president of different sexes or communities; local head/deputy and Dalit women's seats; **2026**: **96 of 275** (~35%), only **14** via FPTP; 2017 local: ~14,000 women (~41%); Bhandari president **2015**; **Sushila Karki** interim PM **2025**.
- **Lessons for Pakistan** — design beats culture; direct election; local quotas as training; intersectional quotas; dynasty ≠ access; enforcement.


- **Pakistan as a case** — firsts at the top (Fatima Jinnah **1965**; Benazir **1988**; Maryam Nawaz **2024**); quota that came and went (10, 6, 10, 20, none, 60); laws made by women (2010–2016); persistent gaps (9.94m voters, 43% vs 52%, 11 general seats, zero ministers 2025). Pakistan sits in the **middle**: ahead of India and Sri Lanka on women's share, behind Nepal, similar to Bangladesh on indirect reserved seats.
- **Common challenges** — dynastic politics; party gatekeeping (Nepal only 14 FPTP winners, Bangladesh 7 direct); money and violence; proxies (*sarpanch-pati*); norms of purdah, honour and care; the local–national gap.
- **Lesson in one line** — quotas get women into office; **party reform, money, safety and social change** decide whether they gain power.
- **Three-sentence comparison for any quota answer** — "Nepal's constitutional one-third with PR lists produced 35% women in 2026; India's panchayat quota showed women leaders change spending; Bangladesh shows women at the top do not guarantee women on general seats."

## 12. Debates in one line each

- **Direct vs indirect reserved seats** — move towards direct election.
- **5% tickets** — raise, place in winnable seats, sanction.
- **Quota as ceiling** — make it a floor.
- **Dynastic women** — a route, not a disqualification; create other routes.
- **Do women represent women?** — united on violence and rights; poor and minority women still missing.
- **Participation as a human right (2024)** — yes (UDHR 21, ICCPR 25, CEDAW 7, Arts 17, 25, 34); the state must remove barriers.
- **Zero women ministers (2025)** — appointments are the fastest fix; elected women are more durable.
- **Human-rights basis (2024)** — UDHR 21; ICCPR 25 (Pakistan **2010**); CEDAW 7 and 4(1) (Pakistan **1996**); **General Comment 25 (1996)**: states must take positive measures against obstacles (illiteracy, poverty, restrictions on movement); SDG **5.5**; Arts 17, 25, 32, 34, 51, 59, 106; a right that half the citizens cannot use is a right only on paper.
- **Mainstreaming (2021)** — the goal is women in general seats, cabinets, party leadership and every policy field, as **gender mainstreaming** (Beijing 1995) intends — not a reserved minority in a separate box.
